# Every text file is checked out with LF endings, on Windows too. Tests hold
# files to each other byte for byte (the committed MCP launcher to the
# installers' copy, among others), and a checkout with
# core.autocrlf failed them for its line endings alone. The rules below say
# why some folders need LF wherever they are checked out, and crew/ none.
* text=auto eol=lf
# crew/ is a byte-for-byte copy of a tagged crew release, pinned by sha256 in
# crew/crew.lock.json. A checkout that converted its line endings (Windows with
# core.autocrlf) would fail the lock test, and cli/sync-hooks.mjs, which runs on
# every workspace install, would refuse to build the CLI package.
crew/** -text
# Any shell script has to reach Linux with LF endings, from a Windows checkout
# or from git archive run there: bash reads a CR as part of the line.
*.sh text eol=lf
# cli/, and the hooks/, mcp/, clients/ and digest/ that cli/sync-hooks.mjs copies into
# it, are what `npx agentmash` ships: a stranger's init commits those hooks
# beside the ones the web installer commits from the server, so they reach
# every platform with LF endings. 0.12.0 was packed from a Windows checkout
# and shipped CRLF; the sync now refuses to pack a package that has any.
cli/** text=auto eol=lf
hooks/** text=auto eol=lf
mcp/** text=auto eol=lf
clients/** text=auto eol=lf
digest/** text=auto eol=lf
